************** G E N E R A L   I N F O R M A T I O N *************9237 1451   66
                                                                  9237 1451   67
This evaluated data file is based primarily on the nuclear model  9237 1451   68
code Talys [kon07], version 1.4 and on existing resonance         9237 1451   69
parameter tables. It is part of a large collection of isotopic    9237 1451   70
evaluations, all created by running Talys with either default or  9237 1451   71
adjusted input parameters. This means that the data in this       9237 1451   72
evaluation have been tested in detail for some isotopes against   9237 1451   73
individual experimental data, while for other isotopes it is      9237 1451   74
only as good as the global quality of Talys at the present moment.9237 1451   75
The mutual quality of all individual evaluations is however       9237 1451   76
consistent: The same set of nuclear models is used and, equally   9237 1451   77
important, the same ENDF-6 formatting procedures for each isotope.9237 1451   78
The resulting data file provides a complete representation of     9237 1451   79
nuclear data needed for transport, damage, heating, radioactivity,9237 1451   80
and shielding applications over the incident neutron energy range 9237 1451   81
from 1.0E-11 to 200 MeV.                                          9237 1451   82
                                                                  9237 1451   83
For the resonance range we have adopted the best possible         9237 1451   84
resonance parameters from available numerical tables or from an   9237 1451   85
existing library.                                                 9237 1451   86
                                                                  9237 1451   87
All transport data for neutrons, charged particles, photons and   9237 1451   88
residual nuclides are filed using a combination of MF1,2,3,4,5,6, 9237 1451   89
12,14,15. This includes cross sections, angular distributions,    9237 1451   90
double-differential spectra, discrete and continuum photon        9237 1451   91
production cross sections, and residual production (activation)   9237 1451   92
cross sections. Moreover, isomeric production data are stored in  9237 1451   93
MF8,9,10. This evaluation can thus be used as both transport      9237 1451   94
and activation library. The data file has been created            9237 1451   95
automatically using the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.            9237 1451   96
                                                                  9237 1451   97
A complete set of covariance data is included, which is stored    9237 1451   98
in MF32,33,34.                                                    9237 1451   99
                                                                  9237 1451  100
##### ORIGIN                                                      9237 1451  101
                                                                  9237 1451  102
Resonance parameters (MF2/MT151): Best data from existing sets    9237 1451  103
All other data < 200 MeV        : Produced with Talys code        9237 1451  104
                                                                  9237 1451  105
*************************** T H E O R Y **************************9237 1451  106
                                                                  9237 1451  107
Talys is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   9237 1451  108
of nuclear reactions. Talys simulates reactions that involve      9237 1451  109
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    9237 1451  110
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range and for      9237 1451  111
target nuclides of mass 12 and heavier. This is achieved by       9237 1451  112
implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into a single     9237 1451  113
code system. It enables to evaluate nuclear reactions from        9237 1451  114
the unresolved resonance region up to intermediate energies. This 9237 1451  115
evaluation is based on a theoretical analysis that utilizes the   9237 1451  116
optical model, compound nucleus statistical theory, fission,      9237 1451  117
direct reactions and pre-equilibrium processes, in combination    9237 1451  118
with databases and models for nuclear structure. The following    9237 1451  119
output of Talys is stored in this data file:                      9237 1451  120
                                                                  9237 1451  121
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   9237 1451  122
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        9237 1451  123
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     9237 1451  124
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   9237 1451  125
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 9237 1451  126
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                9237 1451  127
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   9237 1451  128
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    9237 1451  129
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        9237 1451  130
- Residual production cross sections                              9237 1451  131
- Recoil data                                                     9237 1451  132
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (n,xn), (n,xp),..           9237 1451  133
- Total particle energy spectra                                   9237 1451  134
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      9237 1451  135
                                                                  9237 1451  136
Here follows a short description of the used nuclear models:      9237 1451  137
                                                                  9237 1451  138
##### OPTICAL MODEL                                               9237 1451  139
                                                                  9237 1451  140
All optical model calculations are performed by ECIS-06 [ray94],  9237 1451  141
in Talys used as a subroutine. The default optical model          9237 1451  142
potentials (OMP) used are the local and global parameterizations  9237 1451  143
of Koning and Delaroche [kon03]. These are phenomenological OMPs  9237 1451  144
for neutrons and protons which in principle are valid over the    9237 1451  145
1 keV - 200 MeV energy range, though the low energy boundary of   9237 1451  146
validity may differ from nucleus to nucleus. For neutrons and     9237 1451  147
protons, the used parameterization is given in Eq. (7) of         9237 1451  148
[kon03]. Revised versions of these potentials (unpublished) are   9237 1451  149
used in the case of deformed nuclides.                            9237 1451  150
To calculate the transmission coefficients and reaction           9237 1451  151
cross sections for deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha          9237 1451  152
particles, we use OMPs that are directly derived from our nucleon 9237 1451  153
potentials using Watanabe's folding approach.                     9237 1451  154
                                                                  9237 1451  155
##### DIRECT REACTIONS                                            9237 1451  156
                                                                  9237 1451  157
The built-in ECIS-06 is used for DWBA or coupled-channels         9237 1451  158
calculations for rotational or vibrational (or a combination of   9237 1451  159
these) nuclides, depending on the information present in the      9237 1451  160
nuclear structure database of Talys.                              9237 1451  161
In addition, a macroscopic, phenomenological model to describe    9237 1451  162
giant resonances in the inelastic channel is used. For each       9237 1451  163
multipolarity an energy weighted sum rule applies and a DWBA      9237 1451  164
calculation with ECIS-06 is performed for each giant resonance    9237 1451  165
state. The cross section is then spread over the continuum with a 9237 1451  166
Gaussian distribution.                                            9237 1451  167
                                                                  9237 1451  168
##### COMPOUND NUCLEUS                                            9237 1451  169
                                                                  9237 1451  170
For binary compound nucleus reactions we use the model of         9237 1451  171
Moldauer, i.e. the Hauser-Feshbach model corrected for width      9237 1451  172
fluctuations. The transmission coefficients have been generated   9237 1451  173
with the aforementioned OMPs and the full j,l-dependence of the   9237 1451  174
transmission coefficients in the Hauser-Feshbach model is used.   9237 1451  175
For each nucleus that can be reached through a binary reaction,   9237 1451  176
several discrete levels and a continuum described by level        9237 1451  177
densities are included simultaneously as competing channels.      9237 1451  178
                                                                  9237 1451  179
The compound nucleus angular distributions are calculated with    9237 1451  180
the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism, leading to compound nucleus       9237 1451  181
Legendre coefficients that are added to their direct              9237 1451  182
counterparts. For multiple compound emission, i.e. emitted        9237 1451  183
particles after the binary emission, we use the Hauser-Feshbach   9237 1451  184
model. Again, for each residual nucleus several discrete states   9237 1451  185
are included as well as a continuum described by level densities. 9237 1451  186
Multiple compound emission is continued until all reaction        9237 1451  187
channels are closed and the population distribution of all        9237 1451  188
residual nuclides is depleted, through gamma decay, until they    9237 1451  189
end up in the ground state or in an isomer.                       9237 1451  190
                                                                  9237 1451  191
For the level density, we take the Constant Temperature Model     9237 1451  192
with level density parameters as given in [kon08]. This employs   9237 1451  193
the temperature law at low energies and a Fermi gas expression at 9237 1451  194
high energies, and takes into account the damping of shell        9237 1451  195
effects at high excitation energy. We have obtained the level     9237 1451  196
density parameters from a simultaneous fit to all experimental    9237 1451  197
discrete levels and D0 values from the RIPL library [rip09].      9237 1451  198
                                                                  9237 1451  199
Gamma-ray transmission coefficients are generated with the        9237 1451  200
Kopecky-Uhl generalized Lorentzian for strength functions, with   9237 1451  201
giant dipole resonance parameters taken from the RIPL library     9237 1451  202
[rip09], and normalized with experimental radiative widths.       9237 1451  203
                                                                  9237 1451  204
##### PRE-EQUILIBRIUM REACTIONS                                   9237 1451  205
                                                                  9237 1451  206
For pre-equilibrium reactions, which become important for         9237 1451  207
incident energies above about 10 MeV, we use the two-component    9237 1451  208
exciton model [kon04], in which the neutron or proton types of    9237 1451  209
particles and holes are followed throughout the reaction. For     9237 1451  210
energies above 20 MeV, multiple pre-equilibrium emission up to    9237 1451  211
any order of particle emission was included in the calculations.  9237 1451  212
A parameterization for the squared matrix element is used that is 9237 1451  213
valid for the whole energy range of this evaluation.              9237 1451  214
                                                                  9237 1451  215
For deuterons, tritons, helions and alpha-particles, an extra     9237 1451  216
contribution was added from the pick/up and knock-out reaction    9237 1451  217
model by Kalbach [kal05].                                         9237 1451  218
                                                                  9237 1451  219
For photons, the model of Akkermans and Gruppelaar [akk85] was    9237 1451  220
applied, to simulate the direct and semi-direct capture           9237 1451  221
processes.                                                        9237 1451  222
                                                                  9237 1451  223
The angular distribution systematics by Kalbach [kal88] were used 9237 1451  224
to describe the angular distributions for all continuum           9237 1451  225
particles. An isotopic distribution for photons was adopted.      9237 1451  226
                                                                  9237 1451  227
***************** F I L E   I N F O R M A T I O N ****************9237 1451  228
                                                                  9237 1451  229
##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    9237 1451  230
                                                                  9237 1451  231
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       9237 1451  232
                                                                  9237 1451  233
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          9237 1451  234
                                                                  9237 1451  235
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   9237 1451  236
                                                                  9237 1451  237
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 9237 1451  238
                                                                  9237 1451  239
If present, we adopt the resonance parameters from an existing    9237 1451  240
table or library. The choice of library is made, for each isotope 9237 1451  241
separately, on basis of completeness, date of the evaluation and  9237 1451  242
used representation. If not present, the potential scattering     9237 1451  243
radius, calculated from the optical model, is written to MF2.     9237 1451  244
The default is to take the resonances from a revised version of   9237 1451  245
the Atlas of Neutron Resonances, 5th edition [mug06].             9237 1451  246
                                                                  9237 1451  247
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                9237 1451  248
                                                                  9237 1451  249
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       9237 1451  250
calculated with Talys. If the maximal cross section in an         9237 1451  251
excitation function over the whole energy range does not exceed   9237 1451  252
1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not included at all. Cross sections     9237 1451  253
lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have no physical meaning and   9237 1451  254
are set to zero. We present only the most important reactions     9237 1451  255
explicitly with a separate MT-number. The minor reaction channels 9237 1451  256
are also present but they are stored in MT5 and can be            9237 1451  257
reproduced in combination with MF6. All MT numbers extend up to   9237 1451  258
the highest incident energy.                                      9237 1451  259
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:9237 1451  260
                                                                  9237 1451  261
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  9237 1451  262
                                                                  9237 1451  263
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    9237 1451  264
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     9237 1451  265
                                                                  9237 1451  266
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     9237 1451  267
                                                                  9237 1451  268
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    9237 1451  269
total cross section.                                              9237 1451  270
                                                                  9237 1451  271
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            9237 1451  272
                                                                  9237 1451  273
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    9237 1451  274
                                                                  9237 1451  275
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        9237 1451  276
                                                                  9237 1451  277
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  9237 1451  278
                                                                  9237 1451  279
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           9237 1451  280
                                                                  9237 1451  281
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   9237 1451  282
MT-number. As the incident energy increases, the cross section    9237 1451  283
of MT5 increases as more reaction channels are no longer stored   9237 1451  284
under a specific MT number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be     9237 1451  285
combined with MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross         9237 1451  286
sections particle production cross sections and                   9237 1451  287
(double-)differential cross sections.                             9237 1451  288
                                                                  9237 1451  289
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 9237 1451  290
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 9237 1451  291
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 9237 1451  292
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 9237 1451  293
- MT51-70  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-20th excited state      9237 1451  294
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       9237 1451  295
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              9237 1451  296
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  9237 1451  297
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  9237 1451  298
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  9237 1451  299
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  9237 1451  300
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  9237 1451  301
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       9237 1451  302
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        9237 1451  303
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9237 1451  304
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        9237 1451  305
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9237 1451  306
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        9237 1451  307
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        9237 1451  308
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        9237 1451  309
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       9237 1451  310
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        9237 1451  311
                                                                  9237 1451  312
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           9237 1451  313
                                                                  9237 1451  314
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    9237 1451  315
discrete levels.                                                  9237 1451  316
                                                                  9237 1451  317
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         9237 1451  318
                                                                  9237 1451  319
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  9237 1451  320
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   9237 1451  321
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      9237 1451  322
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  9237 1451  323
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      9237 1451  324
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         9237 1451  325
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   9237 1451  326
on an angular grid.                                               9237 1451  327
                                                                  9237 1451  328
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       9237 1451  329
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        9237 1451  330
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9237 1451  331
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9237 1451  332
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         9237 1451  333
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        9237 1451  334
                                                                  9237 1451  335
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     9237 1451  336
                                                                  9237 1451  337
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     9237 1451  338
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   9237 1451  339
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  9237 1451  340
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             9237 1451  341
All data are generated with Talys.                                9237 1451  342
                                                                  9237 1451  343
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   9237 1451  344
                                                                  9237 1451  345
MT5 contains the production yields of particles and residual      9237 1451  346
products. It also contains the secondary energy-angle             9237 1451  347
distributions for all particles and photons which are not         9237 1451  348
represented by explicit MT numbers. First, the yields for         9237 1451  349
neutrons are given for the whole energy range. Next, on a         9237 1451  350
secondary energy grid the relative emission spectra are given     9237 1451  351
together with the parameters for the Kalbach systematics for      9237 1451  352
angular distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for    9237 1451  353
discrete states have been broadened and added to the continuum    9237 1451  354
spectra. This procedure is repeated for protons, deuterons,       9237 1451  355
tritons, Helium-3, alpha particles and photons. Finally, the      9237 1451  356
residual production yields are given per final product. All these 9237 1451  357
yields and relative distributions can be multiplied with the      9237 1451  358
cross sections given in MF3/MT5 to get the production cross       9237 1451  359
sections and (double-)differential cross sections.                9237 1451  360
                                                                  9237 1451  361
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     9237 1451  362
                                                                  9237 1451  363
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  9237 1451  364
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 9237 1451  365
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          9237 1451  366
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       9237 1451  367
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 9237 1451  368
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     9237 1451  369
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  9237 1451  370
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      9237 1451  371
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         9237 1451  372
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             9237 1451  373
                                                                  9237 1451  374
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   9237 1451  375
followed:                                                         9237 1451  376
                                                                  9237 1451  377
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9237 1451  378
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9237 1451  379
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         9237 1451  380
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     9237 1451  381
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9237 1451  382
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9237 1451  383
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9237 1451  384
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9237 1451  385
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     9237 1451  386
                                                                  9237 1451  387
